   703	   `:connect!`.  The target is exactly one of `:name`, resolved and
   704	   attached by `dial-step`, or `:identities`, a non-empty vector of
   705	   distinct identities attached at once, here; anything else is
   706	   `{:status :refused :reason ::invalid-target}`.  `:bounds` merges over
   707	   `production-bounds`; `:events`, optional, is the link's event writer.
   708	   Every dial is fresh: its own traffic medium, attacher, cursor and
   709	   channel ring.
   710	
   711	   A name dial answers `{:status :resolving :spec :descriptor :name
   712	   :policy :dial :handle :handles :identity :outcome :since}`, `:policy`
   713	   the link policy and `:since` the first step's `now`.
   714	
   715	   An identities dial takes `:now`, the driver's clock reading at
   716	   dialing: it is recorded on the link before the channel is
   717	   established, so the probes sent at once carry `give-up-after`
   718	   deadlines and a connection that never opens is lost at them (without
   719	   `:now` nothing expires, acceptable only for tests).  It answers
   720	   `:status :attached` with `:handles {identity reflection}` and
   721	   `:handle nil` -- with a writer among the reflections there is no
   722	   \"the\" handle -- or `:status :lost` with the attacher's own
   723	   `:outcome` (transport-error for a local reachability failure,
   724	   invalid-descriptor).
   725	
   726	   Without `:connect!`, or for a transport other than :ws, `{:status
